`-deps` no longer causes spurious `-betterC` errors in unreferenced modules

Using `-deps` together with `-betterC` previously caused spurious errors
in transitively imported druntime modules the user never referenced —
`throw` statements in `core.exception` and GC array literals in
`core.time` being the most common.

This happened because `-deps` triggered `semantic3OnDependencies`, which
forced full function-body analysis on every transitively imported module.
Under `-betterC`, that analysis flags constructs that are perfectly legal
in druntime but illegal in user code.

The `-deps` output is populated during normal semantic passes and does not
require this additional analysis step, so the call has been removed.

To get recursive dependencies (the original motivation for this behavior),
combine `-deps` with `-i`.
